  "response": "## What the graph actually says\n\nPulling the three detector families out of the 92 findings:\n\n**Connectivity (`high_fan_in_fan_out`)** — repo cutoffs are fan-in p95 = 9 / p99 = 10, fan-out p95 = 6 / p99 = 11:\n\n| File | fan-in | fan-out | transitive importers | note from evidence |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `src/utils.ts` | **18** | 1 | 29 | 1.8× the p99 fan-in cutoff |\n| `src/index.ts` | 4 | **17** | 7 | 1.5× the p99 fan-out cutoff |\n| `src/esbuild/index.ts` | 1 | 11 | 7 | exactly at p99 fan-out |\n| `src/plugin.ts` | 10 | 3 | 29 | 9 of 10 importers type-only |\n| `src/log.ts` | 9 | 0 | **30** | 4 of 9 type-only; at p95 |\n| `test/utils.ts` | 9 | 0 | 9 | test harness |\n| `src/rollup.ts` | 0 | 7 | **0** | nothing imports it |\n| `src/api-extractor.ts` | 1 | 6 | 7 | exactly at p95 fan-out |\n\n**Large files:** `src/index.ts` 459 (1.5×), `src/utils.ts` 424 (1.4×), `schema.json` 362 (1.2×).\n\n**Top `large_function` (high severity):** `index.ts::build` 293 (4.9×), `index.ts::<anonymous>` 274, `esbuild/index.ts::runEsbuild` 237 (4.0×), `index.ts::mainTasks` 194, `cli-main.ts::main` 153 (2.5×), `esbuild/postcss.ts::postcssPlugin` 140, `postcss.ts::setup` 126, `rollup.ts::getRollupConfig` 122.\n\nOne thing to read carefully before ordering: `build` (167–459) ⊃ `<anonymous>` (183–456) ⊃ `mainTasks` (260–453) are nested line ranges. That's **one** 293-line region reported three times, not three independent god functions. Same for `postcssPlugin` ⊃ `setup` and `sveltePlugin` ⊃ `setup` ⊃ `<anonymous>`. Treating them as separate work items would triple-count the effort.\n\n## Triage order\n\n### 1. `src/utils.ts` — the only genuine blast-radius play\n\nFan-in 18 against a p99 cutoff of 10, and 29 transitive importers — roughly 59% of the 49 JS files reach it. `blast_radius` 0.46, the highest in the report. It's also a 424-line God File, so those 18 consumers are each pulling in a module whose contents they mostly don't use.\n\nThe refactor pays three times over, because two more findings sit on the same file at the same 0.46 blast radius:\n- `crime_00027` — `jsoncParse` catches with no binding and returns `{}`, so 18 direct consumers can't distinguish \"empty config\" from \"config failed to parse\".\n- `crime_00012` — 7 sync I/O calls across `removeFiles`, `ensureTempDeclarationDir`, and others.\n\nSplitting by responsibility (fs/removal helpers, jsonc parsing, path+name helpers) turns one 18-consumer hub into three 2–8-consumer modules. Do it behind a re-export shim from `utils.ts` first so the 18 import sites move in a separate mechanical commit — the split itself is a `risk: medium` action per the finding, and doing structure and call-site churn in one commit is how these go wrong.\n\n### 2. `src/index.ts` — highest concentration, but for comprehension, not ripple\n\nFan-out 17 (1.5× p99), 459 lines, and it owns six `large_function` findings plus the `build`/`buildAll` False Identity pair. But fan-in is only 4 and transitive reach is 7 — **this file does not ripple**. Its `blast_radius` is 0.28, less than half of `utils.ts`.\n\nSo the justification here isn't blast radius, it's that this is where change *lands*: a 293-line orchestrator body that an agent cannot hold in context, importing 17 modules. Extract `normalizeOptions` (96), `startWatcher` (79), and `buildAll` (75) into siblings; that alone drops the file well under threshold and pulls a chunk of the 17 imports out with them. Second, not first, precisely because fixing it helps editors of this file and nobody else.\n\n### 3. `src/plugin.ts::buildFinished` — the function, not the module\n\nFan-in 10 sits at the p99 cutoff, but the detector's own evidence disqualifies the module-level fix: *\"9 of 10 importers take types only — a shared type module's coupling is compile-time, and being depended on is its job.\"* Splitting it would be churn against a correct design.\n\nWhat is worth doing is `buildFinished` (`crime_00039`): 103 lines, `blast_radius` 0.45, 29 transitive importers, `test_gap` 0.75. It's the one runtime surface behind an otherwise type-only module. Extract its body; leave the exports alone.\n\n### 4. `src/esbuild/index.ts::runEsbuild` — biggest single function, contained\n\n237 lines at 4.0× threshold, reading 38 distinct properties off one `options` bag, in a file with fan-out 11. Fan-in is 1 and `blast_radius` is 0.25 — the damage is confined. Genuinely worth doing, but nothing downstream gets safer when it lands.\n\n### 5. `src/cli-main.ts::main` — high severity, 2 importers\n\n153 lines at 2.5×, `blast_radius` 0.15. High severity because of size; near-zero reach because it's the CLI entrypoint. Cheap to fix, low payoff.\n\n## Leave alone\n\n**`src/log.ts`** — 30 transitive importers, the *widest reach in the repo*, and it should not be touched. Fan-in 9 is exactly at the p95 cutoff (i.e. inside the tier, not above it), fan-out is 0, and 4 of 9 importers take types only. A logger imported by 9 modules is a logger doing its job; it's a stable leaf with nothing to split into. Reach is not risk when the module has no dependencies of its own. The only finding against it is two unprefixed booleans (`onlyImportant`, `silent`) — a rename, not a refactor.\n\n**`src/rollup.ts`** — fan-out 7, but fan-in 0 and `blast_radius` 0.0 with **0 transitive importers**. Nothing in the scanned graph depends on it, so no refactor here reduces blast radius by definition. It does carry the report's single highest `agent_risk` (0.49, `crime_00004`: `getRollupConfig` calls `setSilent` and `removeFiles` behind a `get*` name) — worth a five-minute rename on agent-safety grounds, but keep it out of the blast-radius conversation.\n\n**`src/api-extractor.ts`** — fan-out 6 is *exactly* the p95 cutoff, fan-in 1. It's in the tier only by the boundary rule. Nothing here justifies restructuring.\n\n**`test/utils.ts`** — fan-in 9 from 9 test files is test infrastructure being reused correctly. The unbounded `Promise.all` over `Object.keys(files)` (`crime_00056`, confidence 0.88) is real but bounded by fixture size in a test harness.\n\n**`schema.json`** — a 362-line God File finding on a declarative JSON schema, `blast_radius` 0. \"Split by responsibility\" is not a meaningful operation on a schema document. Add it to the config exclusions rather than carrying it as debt.\n\n## Two caveats on this ranking\n\n**`churn` and `recency` are 0 on all 92 findings.** There's no git history signal in this scan, so the entire order is static coupling. In a real repo, churn would be the tiebreaker — a 200-line function nobody has touched in two years is not the same risk as a 90-line one edited weekly, and that could reorder items 2–5. Worth re-running with history available before committing to the sequence.\n\n**The `high_fan_in_fan_out` detector is advisory, not prescriptive.** All eight instances land at confidence 0.70 with `agent_risk` between 0.19 and 0.30, and every one carries the same suggested action: `treat_as_load_bearing` — *read the importers list, prefer additive changes*. The generic `fix_shape` (\"split or invert\") is the charge's boilerplate, not a per-file recommendation. Of the eight, only `src/utils.ts` has fan-in far enough past p99 to make splitting the right call; for the rest, \"treat as load-bearing\" is the whole finding.",
